From 2d1adf73841d8e23814a2777926713d1b6bf7504 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 04:04:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(authz): enforce resource scope so the 'own' permission
 level means something

AuthorizeService accepted a $resource argument and never read it, and no call
site passed one. Every level containing 'O' was collapsed to a plain allow:

    if ($level === 'F' || $level === 'R' || str_contains($level, 'O'))

so 'own' was documentation, not enforcement. TeachAccessService and
OfferingAccessService scoped by hand, but the /admin/* routes wrapping the same
services were guarded only by role-level permission middleware. Any Instructor
could lock, submit or reopen grades, add components, author assessments, import
attendance or moderate discussions for ANY offering in the school.

What 'own' means depends on the role, which a level alone cannot express: for a
student it is 'on my own behalf', for an instructor it is 'an offering I staff'.
config/permission_scopes.php makes that explicit - 14 offering-scoped keys, and
the roles (INSTRUCTOR, TA) whose grants those keys confine. Self-scoped keys
such as profile.edit_own, finance.pay and assignments.submit are deliberately
absent and keep working without a resource.

- ResourceScopeResolver maps a resource to its offering(s) and checks
  offering_staff. Resolution is explicit per model, so a new offering-owned
  model must opt in rather than silently inherit access.
- AuthorizeService resolves the granting role: an unscoped grant from any role
  wins outright, so an Academic Admin who also teaches is not confined. A
  scoped grant on a scoped key requires a resource and fails closed without one.
- RequirePermission now hands the authorizer the route-bound model, which fixes
  every /admin/* route at once with no changes to routes/web.php.
- 20 service call sites thread their resource through.

Existing fixtures created instructors holding the role but staffed on nothing,
which is why they passed before. They now staff explicitly via a shared
TestCase::staffOffering() helper - that is the corrected setup, not a workaround.

ResourceScopeTest is the invariant suite: 11 cross-offering operations denied,
the same operations allowed on a staffed offering, admins unaffected, TA denied
the lock even on their own offering, fail-closed without a resource, and
self-scoped 'own' permissions still working.

Verified: 131 -> 139 passing, 608 assertions; the scope suite fails 4/8 against
the pre-change code with 'Expected [gradebook.addComponent] to be denied across
offerings, but it was allowed'; pint clean; PostgreSQL 16 migrate:fresh --seed
green.
